# Blessed root scratch dir for probes / experiments / one-off investigations.
# Put ad-hoc .bz probes and compiled probe binaries HERE (or in
# /var/tmp/bezel-scratch) — never loose in the repo root. See CLAUDE.md.
# scripts/gc_scratch.sh auto-quarantines strays that still land in the root.
/scratch/

# Local scheduler lock files (per-session)
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# ── lit's in-tree build output ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# MEASURED, with a real bezelc on PATH and the repo's own lit.cfg.py (lit
# 18.1.8): a hand-run `lit tests/lit` leaves 2323 files under tests/lit/ —
#   1127 EXTENSIONLESS objects named for the row stem, one per row whose
#   RUN line compiles without -o, sitting NEXT TO THAT ROW'S SOURCE;
#   1196 dotted files: .lit_test_times.txt plus %t scratch under **/Output/.
# The dotted half is suppressed by two explicit rules below. The extensionless
# half is the 1127, and the rule that used to stand here — `tests/lit/*[!.]` —
# is exactly what suppressed them. It has been replaced, not deleted.
#
# WHY a `-o`-less RUN line writes an extensionless file beside its source.
# bezelc fills an empty outputPath with defaultOutput(inputPath)
# (src/main.cpp:254), and defaultOutput strips the directory AND the final
# extension — src/main.cpp:96-107 — so `%bezelc -c %s` emits a file called
# after the row's stem with NO extension, relative to the CWD. lit runs each
# test with its cwd inside test_exec_root, and lit.cfg.py used to fall back to
# test_exec_root = test_source_root, so cwd was the source directory. 2463 RUN
# lines invoke %bezelc with no -o. (`-c` does not skip this: line 254 runs
# before the compile branch at 259-260.)
#
# The rules below suppress exactly the extensionless half: everything under
# tests/lit, minus every directory (so git still descends), minus every name
# CONTAINING A DOT (every source and config: lit.cfg.py:31 makes `.bzl` the
# only lit test suffix, so a lit source always has a dot and can never be
# swallowed). The two trailing rules re-suppress lit's own Output subtree and
# test-times file, which the `*.*` re-inclusion would otherwise expose.
#
# The `tests/lit/*[!.]` rule this replaces read as "a name containing no dot"
# and is not: git matches a gitignore glob against the path's BASENAME and lets
# `*` cross `/`, so `[!.]` tested only the LAST CHARACTER. `row.bzl` ends in `l`,
# so every new lit source matched, and because git does not descend into an
# excluded directory it matched at EVERY depth — a row written into
# tests/lit/sema/ was swallowed too. All 2513 tracked tests/lit/**.bzl files
# were force-added past it, which is why the tracked-row count never revealed
# the hole (#6523).
#
# On git 2.55 `git add tests/lit/<row>.bzl` is NOT silent: it prints "the
# following paths are ignored" and exits 1 (and for a subdir it names the
# DIRECTORY, tests/lit/sema, as ignored). The hazard is that the row is simply
# absent from the commit while `git status` never mentions it, and that one
# non-zero path in a multi-path `git add` is easy to lose.
#
# BELT AND BRACES. lit.cfg.py no longer falls back to test_exec_root =
# test_source_root — it uses build/lit-exec-root, outside the source tree — so
# a hand-run no longer writes any of this into tests/lit/ in the first place.
# These rules cover the remaining paths in: an older lit.cfg.py, a hand-run
# from a worktree predating that change, or a future artifact shape. One that
# slips through surfaces as an untracked file under tests/, where
# scripts/untracked_guard.sh FAILs the gate LOUDLY rather than silently.
tests/lit/**/*
!tests/lit/**/
!tests/lit/**/*.*
/tests/lit/.lit_test_times.txt
tests/lit/**/Output/
tests/lit/**/*.bzl.tmp
tests/lit/**/*.bzl.tmp.*
/tmp*
